# Target C-II — global-turn control of shallow-tooth apex multiplicity

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Repository base:** `befa2362ed20bfdbd8bbda7fa1ef65aefdb5aa11`  
**Status:** proved proof-only strengthening of the shallow-tooth branch; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** Type-II two-edge outer teeth in the fully force-visible C-II core  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The earlier shallow-tooth apex theorem imposed `alpha_eta<pi/12` in order to make the
apex map pointwise injective. That restriction is stronger than necessary for a linear
count. Convex-hull turning gives a global substitute.

For every threshold satisfying

\[
\alpha_\eta:=\arccos(1-\eta/2)<\pi/6,
\]

an apex can support at most two shallow teeth; if it supports two, their loaded bases
are adjacent force-hull edges and the shared hull corner has exterior turn greater
than `2pi/3`. Hence at most two apices are duplicated in the entire loaded hull.

This permits a substantially larger shallow threshold and improves the tooth-branch
penetration density.

## 1. Unit-circle geometry at one apex

Let `x` be the unloaded apex of an `eta`-shallow two-edge outer tooth with loaded base
`ab`. Thus

\[
|x-a|=|x-b|=1,
\qquad
|a-b|\ge2-\eta.
\]

The smaller central angle at `x` satisfies

\[
\angle axb\ge\pi-2\alpha_\eta.
\tag{GT.1}
\]

Every two distinct contact neighbors of `x` have circular angular separation at least
`pi/3`, by unit separation.

Assume from now on

\[
\boxed{\alpha_\eta<\pi/6.}
\tag{GT.2}
\]

## 2. Two disjoint shallow bases cannot share one apex

Suppose two shallow tooth bases `ab` and `cd` have the same apex `x` and four distinct
loaded endpoints. Both bases are edges of the same convex loaded force hull, so they
do not cross. Their endpoint pairs therefore do not alternate around the unit circle
centered at `x`.

After cyclic relabeling, write the four endpoints as `a,b,c,d`, with bases `ab` and
`cd`. Let the consecutive central gaps be

\[
g_{ab},g_{bc},g_{cd},g_{da}.
\]

Unit separation gives every gap at least `pi/3`. Hence each gap is at most `pi`, since
the other three already sum to at least `pi`. Therefore the smaller central angles of
the two bases are exactly `g_ab` and `g_cd`, and (GT.1) gives

\[
g_{ab},g_{cd}\ge\pi-2\alpha_\eta.
\]

Consequently

\[
2\pi
\ge2(\pi-2\alpha_\eta)+{2\pi\over3},
\]

which contradicts (GT.2). Thus:

\[
\boxed{\text{two shallow bases at one apex must share a loaded endpoint.}}
\tag{GT.3}
\]

For a loaded convex polygon with at least four vertices, three distinct hull edges
cannot be pairwise endpoint-intersecting. It follows immediately from (GT.3) that one
apex supports at most two shallow teeth.

## 3. A duplicated apex consumes more than 120 degrees of hull turn

Suppose the two bases at `x` are the adjacent hull edges `ab` and `ac`, sharing the
loaded hull vertex `a`.

On the unit circle centered at `x`, both `b` and `c` lie within angular distance
`2 alpha_eta` of the antipode of `a`. Hence their smaller central separation `gamma`
satisfies

\[
\gamma\le4\alpha_\eta< {2\pi\over3}<\pi.
\tag{GT.4}
\]

The short arc from `b` to `c` does not contain `a`. By the inscribed-angle theorem,
the interior angle of the loaded force hull at `a` is

\[
\angle bac={\gamma\over2}\le2\alpha_\eta.
\tag{GT.5}
\]

Therefore the exterior hull turn `phi_a` obeys

\[
\boxed{
\phi_a=\pi-\angle bac
\ge\pi-2\alpha_\eta
>{2\pi\over3}.
}
\tag{GT.6}
\]

The exterior turns of a convex polygon sum to `2pi`. Hence there are at most two hull
vertices at which such a duplication can occur.

A fixed pair of adjacent strict hull edges determines at most one common unit-radius
apex: the three noncollinear loaded endpoints have a unique circumcenter. Thus distinct
duplicated apices give distinct high-turn hull vertices. We have proved the finite
multiplicity statement:

### Theorem GT.1 — all but two shallow teeth have distinct apices

For `q>=4` and `alpha_eta<pi/6`, if `N_sh(eta)` is the number of `eta`-shallow
two-edge outer teeth and `A_sh(eta)` the number of distinct tooth apices, then

\[
\boxed{
A_{sh}(\eta)\ge N_{sh}(\eta)-2.
}
\tag{GT.7}
\]

No asymptotic or stress-weight hypothesis is used in the multiplicity proof.

## 4. Penetration contacts

The previous shallow-tooth penetration geometry applies at every such unloaded apex.
Canonical C26 gives full contact degree at least three, and triangle-freeness plus the
supporting loaded base force an additional unit contact strictly inward from the base.
Its inward normal component is greater than

\[
Y_\eta:=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha_\eta)>0.
\tag{GT.8}
\]

Choose one such extra contact at every distinct shallow apex. An undirected contact
edge can be selected from at most its two endpoints. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
E_{pen}(\eta)
\ge {A_{sh}(\eta)\over2}
\ge {1\over2}\left(N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}-2\right).
}
\tag{GT.9}
\]

The finite additive `-1` in the edge count is negligible after division by `D`.

## 5. Current low-`t` numerical strengthening

At the current proof-only floor

\[
t_{42}=C_{146}+0.0013751,
\]

the low-`t` squeeze and tooth alternative give

\[
{d_{out}\over D}\le0.002883975+o(1),
\]

\[
{N_2\over D}\ge
0.4363893718701161781\ldots-o(1).
\]

Choose the exact threshold

\[
\boxed{\eta={4\over15}.}
\]

Then

\[
\cos\alpha_\eta={13\over15}>{\sqrt3\over2},
\]

so `alpha_eta<pi/6` with strict margin. The detour deletion costs only

\[
{1\over\eta}{d_{out}\over D}
\le0.01081490625+o(1).
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
{N_{sh}(4/15)\over D}
\ge0.4255744656201161781\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{GT.10}
\]

By (GT.7), the same normalized lower bound holds for distinct apices. Equation (GT.9)
then gives

\[
\boxed{
{E_{pen}(4/15)\over D}
\ge0.2127872328100580890\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{GT.11}
\]

The inward component has the exact form

\[
\boxed{
Y_{4/15}
={13\sqrt3-2\sqrt{14}\over30}
=0.5011115241615840681\ldots .
}
\tag{GT.12}
\]

This strengthens the previous `0.1965648734D` tooth-branch penetration population to
`0.2127872328D`, while retaining a uniform inward component greater than `0.5011`.

## 6. Effect on the low-`t` frontier

The current physical tooth/long-side alternative becomes

```text
tooth branch:
    >= 0.2127872328 D-o(D) distinct inward contacts,
    each with inward component > 0.5011115;

long-side branch:
    >= 0.2074315447 D-o(D) distinct near-tangent outer contacts,
    or the sharper straight-event subdichotomy.
```

Thus the common branch-independent physical-contact floor is now controlled by the
long-side branch, not by shallow-tooth multiplicity.

This still does not close C-II. The gain is structural: repeated shallow-tooth apices
can no longer absorb any linear fraction of the mandatory tooth population, even at a
threshold arbitrarily close to `eta=2-sqrt(3)` from below. The remaining obstruction
is the rounded long-side/loaded-degree-two geometry and its coupling to the Woodall
diameter thrackle.

## 7. Reproducibility

Run

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_shallow_tooth_global_turn_multiplicity.py
```

for the Decimal constant checks.
